What is the historical context of the Seminole people and their association with black Indians?
The historical context of the Seminole people and their association with black Indians dates back to Spanish Florida, where free blacks, escaped slaves (called maroons), and Seminole Indians allied together in distinct bands. These black Indians, known as Black Seminoles, were mostly blood descendants of Seminole Indians, free blacks, and escaped slaves who had intermarried and formed their own communities. Despite having Seminole lineage, they were often categorized as slaves or freedmen due to the stigma of having dark skin. The Black Seminoles lived alongside the Native American Seminole groups, with some being held as slaves by Seminole leaders, but enjoying more freedom than those held by whites.
